::ALG2_U04_L03_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
What model represents 1200 dollars earning 5% annually?
{
=A. A(t)\=1200(1.05)ᵗ#Correct. The principal is 1200 and the annual growth factor is 1.05.
~B. A(t)\=1200(0.95)ᵗ#This models 5% decrease.
~C. A(t)\=1.05(1200)ᵗ#This reverses principal and factor.
~D. A(t)\=1200+5t#This is linear growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
What factor represents 8% annual interest?
{
~A. 0.08#This is only the rate.
=B. 1.08#Correct. Interest growth uses 1 + 0.08 \= 1.08.
~C. 0.92#This models 8% depreciation.
~D. 1.80#This models 80% growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
What factor represents 12% depreciation?
{
~A. 1.12#This models appreciation.
~B. 0.12#This is the rate lost, not the remaining value.
=C. 0.88#Correct. If 12% is lost, 88% remains.
~D. 1.88#This does not represent 12% depreciation.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
In V(t)\=18000(0.85)ᵗ, what does 0.85 mean?
{
~A. 85% is lost each year.#If 85% were lost, the factor would be 0.15.
~B. The starting value is 0.85.#The starting value is 18000.
~C. The value grows by 85%.#A factor below 1 indicates decay, not growth.
=D. 85% of the value remains each year.#Correct. A factor of 0.85 means the value keeps 85% each period.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
In A(t)\=500(1.03)ᵗ, what is the annual rate?
{
=A. 3% growth#Correct. The factor 1.03 means 1 + 0.03, or 3% growth.
~B. 103% growth#The new amount is 103% of the previous amount, which is 3% growth.
~C. 3% decay#The factor is greater than 1, so it is growth.
~D. 0.03% growth#0.03 as a decimal equals 3%.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
A computer worth 1600 loses 25% each year. Which model fits?
{
~A. V(t)\=1600(1.25)ᵗ#This models appreciation.
=B. V(t)\=1600(0.75)ᵗ#Correct. If 25% is lost, 75% remains.
~C. V(t)\=0.75(1600)ᵗ#This reverses the parameters.
~D. V(t)\=1600-25t#This subtracts 25 dollars each year.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
A savings account starts at 700 and grows by 2% annually. Which model fits?
{
~A. A(t)\=700(0.98)ᵗ#This models 2% decay.
~B. A(t)\=1.02(700)ᵗ#This reverses the parameters.
=C. A(t)\=700(1.02)ᵗ#Correct. The principal is 700 and the growth factor is 1.02.
~D. A(t)\=700+2t#This is linear.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which statement best describes compound interest?
{
~A. Interest is added only once.#That does not describe compounding.
~B. The balance decreases every year.#Compound interest usually models growth unless other payments change the balance.
~C. The rate is ignored.#The rate determines the growth factor.
=D. Interest earns additional interest over time.#Correct. Compound interest applies growth to the updated balance each period.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
A model B(t)\=2500(1.04)ᵗ is evaluated at t\=3. What does the result represent?
{
=A. Balance after 3 time periods#Correct. The input t\=3 gives the amount after three compounding intervals.
~B. The interest rate#The rate is represented by the factor 1.04.
~C. The initial balance#The initial balance is 2500.
~D. The monthly payment#No payment is represented in this model.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which model shows 6% annual depreciation from 30000 dollars?
{
~A. V(t)\=30000(1.06)ᵗ#This models appreciation.
=B. V(t)\=30000(0.94)ᵗ#Correct. Depreciation by 6% means 94% remains.
~C. V(t)\=6(30000)ᵗ#This uses the rate incorrectly.
~D. V(t)\=30000-6t#This is a fixed dollar decrease.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which model shows 6% annual appreciation from 30000 dollars?
{
~A. V(t)\=30000(0.94)ᵗ#This models depreciation.
~B. V(t)\=6(30000)ᵗ#This uses the rate incorrectly.
=C. V(t)\=30000(1.06)ᵗ#Correct. Appreciation by 6% means a factor of 1.06.
~D. V(t)\=30000+6t#This adds 6 dollars each year.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
What does principal mean in a financial model?
{
~A. The annual rate#The rate is the percent change.
~B. The exponent#The exponent represents time periods.
~C. The final amount only#The final amount depends on time and rate.
=D. The starting amount#Correct. Principal is the original amount invested, borrowed, or valued.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which factor shows that an asset keeps 78% of its value each year?
{
=A. 0.78#Correct. Keeping 78% means multiplying by 0.78 each year.
~B. 1.78#This indicates growth.
~C. 0.22#This would mean only 22% remains.
~D. 78#This is not decimal factor form.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
A 4000 dollar balance grows by factor 1.05 for 2 years. Which expression evaluates the balance?
{
~A. 4000(2)¹.05#This reverses time and factor.
=B. 4000(1.05)²#Correct. Use principal times the factor raised to the number of years.
~C. 1.05(4000)²#This reverses principal and factor.
~D. 4000+1.05(2)#This is not compound growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
A 9000 dollar asset is modeled by V(t)\=9000(0.70)ᵗ. What is the depreciation rate?
{
~A. 70%#This is the percent remaining.
~B. 0.70%#This confuses the factor with a percent rate.
=C. 30%#Correct. If 70% remains, 30% is lost each period.
~D. 130%#This does not describe depreciation from factor 0.70.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which is a fair way to compare two investment models?
{
~A. Compare one after 2 years and one after 8 years.#Different time spans are not directly comparable.
~B. Ignore principal because only rates matter.#Starting amount matters.
~C. Ignore rates because only principal matters.#Rates affect growth over time.
=D. Compare both balances after the same number of years.#Correct. Using the same time value supports a fair comparison.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
If A(t)\=1000(1.06)ᵗ, what is A(1)?
{
=A. 1060#Correct. After one year, multiply 1000 by 1.06.
~B. 1006#This adds 6 instead of 6%.
~C. 1600#This treats 6% as 60%.
~D. 6000#This multiplies by 6 instead of 1.06.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
If V(t)\=20000(0.9)ᵗ, what is V(2)?
{
~A. 18000#This is V(1).
=B. 16200#Correct. Compute 20000 times 0.9 squared.
~C. 16000#This subtracts 10% twice from the original instead of compounding.
~D. 2000#This uses only 10% of the starting value.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which factor would produce the fastest growth?
{
~A. 1.02#This grows, but more slowly than 1.12.
~B. 0.98#This is decay.
=C. 1.12#Correct. Among these, 1.12 is the largest growth factor.
~D. 0.88#This is faster decay, not growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L03_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which factor would produce the fastest depreciation?
{
~A. 0.95#This depreciates slowly.
~B. 1.03#This is growth.
~C. 1.20#This is growth.
=D. 0.70#Correct. The smallest positive factor loses the greatest percent each period.
}

